\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Left: tokenized LMs suffer from tokenization bias when prompts end mid-token, as in this code completion example, where the correct continuation \texttt  {a} receives zero probability. Our method uses the same model but predicts the correct byte. Right: our method maps the next-token predictions of arbitrary tokenized LMs to statistically equivalent next-byte predictions (Section~\ref {sec:method}), which enables any ensembling strategy, such as averaging or mixtures of experts.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{2}{Left: tokenized LMs suffer from tokenization bias when prompts end mid-token, as in this code completion example, where the correct continuation \tok {a} receives zero probability. Our method uses the same model but predicts the correct byte. Right: our method maps the next-token predictions of arbitrary tokenized LMs to statistically equivalent next-byte predictions (Section~\ref {sec:method}), which enables any ensembling strategy, such as averaging or mixtures of experts}{figure.1}{}}
